"""Essays for the Learn section. Cases are made up and labeled."""
ESSAYS = [{'body': '<p>Most people in their 30s who stall did nothing wrong. They kept doing what had worked.</p>\n'
          '<p>A career inside a company climbs six steps, and each step pays for something different.</p>\n'
          '<div class="stairs" role="img" aria-label="Six steps: Expertise, Execution, Management, Strategy, Influence, Leadership. Each step pays for something different, and the habit from the '
          'step below gets carried up. Most people get stuck at Management.">\n'
          '  <div class="st s1"><b>Expertise</b><span>Paid to know the answer</span></div>\n'
          '  <div class="st s2"><b>Execution</b><span>Paid to deliver the work</span><i>Carried up: answering every question yourself</i></div>\n'
          '  <div class="st s3"><em>Where most people get stuck</em><b>Management</b><span>Paid to deliver through a team</span><i>Carried up: fixing the work yourself</i></div>\n'
          '  <div class="st s4"><b>Strategy</b><span>Paid to choose which work matters</span><i>Carried up: reporting activity</i></div>\n'
          '  <div class="st s5"><b>Influence</b><span>Paid to move people you do not manage</span><i>Carried up: winning the argument</i></div>\n'
          '  <div class="st s6"><b>Leadership</b><span>Paid to build the people who decide</span><i>Carried up: deciding everything yourself</i></div>\n'
          '</div>\n'
          '<p>Most people I have worked with got stuck on the third step. The trouble is what you carry up. The expert who becomes a lead still answers every question herself. The lead who becomes a '
          'manager still fixes the work at night. The manager who is asked about strategy still sends a list of what the team did.</p>\n'
          '<p>Each of those habits earned the last promotion. That is why they are hard to drop. They feel like the reason you are good at your job.</p>\n'
          '<p>I sat in talent and promotion reviews for more than 100 leaders. Nobody in those rooms argued about who worked hardest. The question was whether the person was already doing what the '
          'next step pays for.</p>\n'
          '<p>Take Meera, a made-up engineering manager with nine years behind her. She reviews every pull request her team writes, and her team ships clean code. Her director sees a strong lead. He '
          'also sees a manager with no time left to say which three of her nine projects should stop.</p>',
  'dek': 'Each level pays for something different. Most people keep doing what the last one paid for.',
  'id': 'e1',
  'shelf': 'Earn more',
  'slug': 'the-habit-that-got-you-promoted',
  'title': 'The habit that got you promoted becomes your ceiling',
  'turn': 'Write down the one thing you still do because nobody does it as well as you. That is likely the habit you carried up. What would you have to trust someone else with, to stop?'},
 {'body': '<p>A promotion is decided in a meeting you do not attend, mostly by people who do not work with you. Your manager speaks for a few minutes. Then the others ask questions.</p>\n'
          '<p>I sat in those meetings for more than 100 leaders across three continents. Hard work hardly came up, because everyone on the list worked hard.</p>\n'
          '<p>Three questions came up again and again.</p>\n'
          '<ol class="list">\n'
          "<li><b>Can anyone say what this person's work changed?</b> A list of what they did was never enough. The room wanted to hear who was better off because of it.</li>\n"
          '<li><b>Have they made a call without all the facts?</b> Or do their decisions travel upward as a list of options for someone else to pick from.</li>\n'
          '<li><b>Do people outside their team help them?</b> A manager is expected to praise their own people. My read is that a voice from another team counts for more.</li>\n'
          '</ol>\n'
          '<p>Years in the role, hours and ratings were all on the page. They rarely settled anything.</p>\n'
          '<p>Your manager can only repeat what you have given them. If your updates are a list of activity, then activity is all they have to say when your name comes up.</p>',
  'dek': 'Promotion reviews ask three questions. None of them is about how hard you work.',
  'id': 'e2',
  'shelf': 'Earn more',
  'slug': 'what-gets-said-about-you',
  'title': 'What gets said about you when you are not in the room',
  'turn': 'Write the three sentences you would want your manager to say about you in that room. Could they say each one today, with a name and a number in it?'},
 {'body': '<p>This is an update a team might send after a good sprint:</p>\n'
          '<p class="quote">"This sprint the team migrated 11 services and closed 42 tickets."</p>\n'
          '<p>It is true, and the team worked hard for it. Now picture the director forwarding it to her own boss. All she can repeat is "they migrated some services".</p>\n'
          '<p>The same sprint, written for the person who has to repeat it:</p>\n'
          '<p class="quote">"Finance now closes the month a day earlier. The remaining four migrations need your vendor-contract decision by Friday."</p>\n'
          '<p>Nothing about the work changed between the two. The second one answers three things a busy reader is asking.</p>\n'
          '<ol class="list">\n'
          '<li><b>Who can now do what?</b> Finance closes a day earlier.</li>\n'
          '<li><b>What number moved?</b> One day, every month.</li>\n'
          '<li><b>What do you need from me, and by when?</b> A decision, by Friday.</li>\n'
          '</ol>\n'
          '<p>The first version makes the reader work out why it matters, and most readers will not do that work. The second can be repeated in a lift.</p>\n'
          '<p>The numbers 11 and 42 are made up.</p>',
  'dek': 'A forwarded update gets ten seconds from someone who has never met you.',
  'id': 'e3',
  'shelf': 'Earn more',
  'slug': 'the-update-your-director-cannot-repeat',
  'title': 'The update your director cannot repeat',
  'turn': 'Take the last update you sent. Underline every verb about your own team: migrated, closed, built. Then rewrite the first line so it starts with someone outside your team.'},
 {'body': '<p>A raise feels like progress. Whether it is depends on what it turned into.</p>\n'
          '<p>Take a made-up household. Before the raise they can reach ₹12 lakh, and their must-pay costs are ₹1,50,000 a month. Twelve lakh divided by one and a half lakh is 8 months of runway. '
          'Then take-home pay goes up by ₹30,000 a month.</p>\n'
          '<p><b>Path one.</b> Within six months the raise has a home. A car EMI of ₹18,500. A bigger flat at ₹8,000 more rent. Small upgrades of ₹3,500. Must-pay costs are now ₹1,80,000. The same '
          '₹12 lakh lasts about 6.7 months. They earn more, and they can last a shorter time.</p>\n'
          '<p><b>Path two.</b> Half the raise becomes new costs. The other ₹15,000 moves out on salary day. Costs are ₹1,65,000. After a year they have added ₹1.8 lakh, so ₹13.8 lakh divided by '
          '₹1.65 lakh is about 8.4 months.</p>\n'
          '<p>The raise was the same in both. One household ends the year at 6.7 months and the other at 8.4. The gap grows every year, because path one has also raised the bar the next raise must '
          'clear.</p>\n'
          '<p>I know path one from the inside. At 33 my income had nearly doubled in three years, and my runway was eight months.</p>\n'
          '<p>There is nothing wrong with the car or the holiday. The point is to choose them knowing what they cost you in months.</p>',
  'dek': 'A raise can make you earn more and last a shorter time. The arithmetic is simple.',
  'id': 'e5',
  'shelf': 'Depend less',
  'slug': 'where-your-last-raise-went',
  'title': 'Where your last raise went',
  'turn': 'Take your last raise, in rupees a month. List where it goes now. How much of it became a cost you cannot stop next month?'},
 {'body': '<p>In 2016 I co-founded a company that ran workshops. We had three months of money. It needed twelve. It closed.</p>\n'
          '<p>The lesson I took from it was to count money in months.</p>\n'
          '<p>The sum is one division:</p>\n'
          '<p class="quote">Money you can reach ÷ must-pay costs each month = runway in months</p>\n'
          '<p><b>Money you can reach</b> is what you could have in your bank account within 30 days: savings, deposits, anything you can turn into cash. Leave out the home you live in and stock that '
          'has not vested.</p>\n'
          '<p><b>Must-pay costs</b> are what your household pays even in a bad month: rent or EMIs, school fees, insurance, groceries, bills.</p>\n'
          '<p>A made-up example: ₹18 lakh you can reach, and ₹1.5 lakh of must-pay costs. That is 12 months.</p>\n'
          '<p>The same ₹18 lakh means very different things in different homes. With costs of ₹3 lakh a month it is 6 months. The rupee figure did not change, and the life it buys halved.</p>\n'
          '<p>Months are also what you feel at work. With 3 months, a hard conversation with your boss puts your home at risk. With 14, you can afford to have it.</p>',
  'dek': 'Rupees tell you how rich you feel. Months tell you how long you can say no.',
  'id': 'e6',
  'shelf': 'Depend less',
  'slug': 'count-your-money-in-months',
  'title': 'Count your money in months',
  'turn': 'Do the division tonight. Then say the number out loud to the other adult in your home, and ask what the two of you would cut first.'},
 {'body': '<p>Picture two managers. They are made up, and they earn the same ₹45 lakh a year. They sit in the same meeting, hearing the same plan from the same boss, and both think it will miss its '
          'date.</p>\n'
          '<p>One has 3 months of runway. The other has 14.</p>\n'
          '<p>The first one nods, and complains to a friend that evening. The second says, "I think this misses the date. This is what I would do instead."</p>\n'
          '<p>The second manager is no braver than the first. A no simply costs him less. For him it is a trade he can price. For the first manager it is a risk to his home.</p>\n'
          '<p>I know the first seat. At 33, with eight months of runway, I sat in a meeting, saw a decision going the wrong way, and said nothing.</p>\n'
          '<p>Now look at what happens next. The manager who speaks up with a recommendation is doing what promotion reviews look for. So his runway raises his level, and his level raises his pay, '
          'which can add to his runway. The two numbers feed each other. That is why I say they multiply.</p>\n'
          '<p>What you do with both is your call. Some people use them to keep climbing. Some build something of their own. Some slow down. Staying where you are counts too, once it is a choice.</p>',
  'dek': 'Two managers, the same pay and the same bad plan. One of them speaks.',
  'id': 'e9',
  'shelf': 'Choose',
  'slug': 'what-runway-changes-in-a-meeting',
  'title': 'What runway changes in a meeting',
  'turn': 'Think of the last meeting where you stayed quiet. At how many months of runway would you have spoken?'},
 {'body': '<p>Take Mitali, a made-up manager at an IT services firm. A product company offers her the work she wants, at lower pay. Her take-home would fall from ₹2.4 lakh a month to ₹1.8 lakh. That '
          'is a 25 percent cut, and everyone she asks says no.</p>\n'
          '<p>Now price it in months.</p>\n'
          "<p>Her household's must-pay costs are ₹1.9 lakh a month. On the new pay she would be ₹10,000 short each month. Over two years, that is ₹2.4 lakh.</p>\n"
          '<p>She has 20 months of runway. At ₹1.9 lakh a month, ₹2.4 lakh is a little over one month of it.</p>\n'
          '<p>So the real question is whether two years in the job she wants are worth about one month of runway. Put that way, most people answer differently than they do to "a 25 percent pay '
          'cut".</p>\n'
          '<p>The sum does not make the decision for her. The new job might not work out. Her costs could rise. It only shows her the true price, and that the price is one she can pay.</p>\n'
          '<p>The same sum gives a very different answer for someone with 3 months of runway. For them the cut would use up most of what they have. The offer is the same, and the right answer is '
          'not.</p>',
  'dek': 'A 25 percent cut sounds reckless. Priced in months, it can cost very little.',
  'id': 'e11',
  'shelf': 'Choose',
  'slug': 'the-pay-cut-you-can-afford',
  'title': 'The pay cut you can afford',
  'turn': 'Think of a move you ruled out because of the pay. Work out the monthly shortfall, multiply by 24, and divide by your must-pay costs. How many months is it?'},
 {'body': '<p>Not everyone in their 30s wants the next title this year. A child arrives, a parent falls ill, or you are simply tired. Slowing down for a while is a fair use of what you have '
          'built.</p>\n'
          '<p>It is only a real option if the numbers hold.</p>\n'
          '<p>Take Shweta, a made-up consulting manager. Her take-home pay is ₹5 lakh a month. She asks to work four days a week for a year, at 80 percent of pay. That is ₹4 lakh a month.</p>\n'
          "<p>Her household's must-pay costs are ₹3.2 lakh. On ₹4 lakh she still covers them, with ₹80,000 to spare. Her runway does not shrink. It only grows more slowly for a year.</p>\n"
          '<p>Now change one number. If her costs were ₹4.5 lakh, the same request would cost her ₹50,000 a month from savings. She could still do it, and she would be paying for it in months.</p>\n'
          '<p>The test is simple. If the lower pay still covers your must-pay costs, a slower year is a choice. If it does not, it is a purchase, and you can price it.</p>\n'
          '<p>Slowing down also has a cost at work that this sum does not show. A year at four days can delay a promotion. I do not know how to put a number on that. It belongs in the decision all '
          'the same.</p>',
  'dek': 'Working less is a real option once lower pay still covers your costs.',
  'id': 'e12',
  'shelf': 'Choose',
  'slug': 'the-slower-year-you-can-choose',
  'title': 'The slower year you can choose',
  'turn': 'What is the lowest monthly pay that still covers your must-pay costs? How far below your current pay is it?'},
 {'body': '<p>Most writing about money and careers ends with someone handing in their notice. Most real stories do not.</p>\n'
          '<p>Take Lokesh, a made-up manager in pharma. He has 22 months of runway. He can name three people outside his company who would take his call. Last month one of them made him an '
          'offer.</p>\n'
          '<p>He turned it down, and stayed where he is.</p>\n'
          '<p>From the outside nothing changed. He has the same desk, the same boss and the same pay. What changed is why he is there. A year ago he stayed because he could not afford to leave. Now '
          'he stays because, having looked at a real offer, he prefers the job he has.</p>\n'
          '<p>That shows up in how he works. He says what he thinks in meetings. He turns down work that does not matter. His boss has noticed, and it is one reason his next review went well.</p>\n'
          '<p>This is what I mean by an Optional Career. The career does not become something you leave. It becomes something you chose, with other choices open.</p>\n'
          '<p>It is also why I do not teach people to quit. Some will. Many will find, once they can afford to go, that they want to stay and do the job differently.</p>',
  'dek': 'The point of runway was never to leave. It is to stay because you want to.',
  'id': 'e13',
  'shelf': 'Choose',
  'slug': 'staying-is-a-choice-too',
  'title': 'Staying is a choice too',
  'turn': 'If you had two years of runway and one good offer in hand, would you still be in your current job on Monday? What would you do differently there?'}]
